#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""register -- read a work register: list it, show one entry, render an index, count it, report
    what has gone quiet, search it for what already exists, and name the next free id.

WHY A COMMAND RATHER THAN A COMMITTED FILE
    A register directory answers "what is the state of everything" only if you open every
    entry. The obvious fix is to generate an index file and commit it -- and that is the shape
    this replaces, for two measured reasons.

    It cannot go stale. A committed index is a derived artifact, and a derived artifact that
    nobody regenerates is worse than none because it is believed. Every drift incident in this
    fleet has that shape.

    And it is far cheaper to consume. Measured on a 218-entry register: the committed index was
    48,840 bytes, the list of open entries 1,409, the status counts 134. For an agent session
    reading into a bounded context that is the difference between twelve thousand tokens and
    three hundred, to answer the same question.

    `index` still exists, because a browsable artifact is a real thing to want -- somebody
    reading the repository on a web UI cannot run a command. It writes on demand rather than
    being a file the repo must keep fresh, and every register renders it the same way instead
    of each one growing its own generator.

THE PREFIX IS DERIVED, NEVER CONFIGURED -- the same rule the register's lint follows. A
    configured prefix is a second place for the answer to live, and the two drift; a derived
    one cannot disagree with the store it describes.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO
NEXT ID IS READ NUMERICALLY, OVER EVERY ENTRY -- closed ones included, because an id is never
    reused. Reading the highest id off a directory listing is the trap this verb replaces: a
    register whose ids are mixed width sorts `X-99` after `X-100` as text, and two sessions
    trusting a lexical listing have allocated the same id in this fleet. The width is derived
    from the widest existing id, like the prefix, so a register padded to four digits keeps
    getting four-digit ids. Mixed widths are reported on stderr, since they are exactly the
    condition under which any other listing of the register gives the wrong answer.

FOLDERS. An entry that is an initiative names its folder as a `folder:` artifact. `list` shows it
    beside the status, and `show` lists the folder's files by type and the decision records its
    README names, so "where is this work, and what is in it" is one command. `health` is the
    on-demand, never-blocking report of what has gone quiet or unowned: an active initiative whose
    folder has had no commit in 30 days, a standing entry past its cadence, and a document in the
    repository-wide type folders that no initiative claims or only one does (a candidate to move
    into it). It is advisory and always exits 0 -- a lint that blocked on staleness would be
    refused by the next person in a hurry, so it reports and leaves the decision.

    The repository root is the nearest ancestor of the register directory holding `.git`. Its
    questions to git run with git's own environment variables removed: inside a git hook, GIT_DIR
    and GIT_INDEX_FILE name the hook's repository and override `-C`, so a register in a snapshot
    directory or another checkout would otherwise be answered from the wrong history.

RELATIONS. An entry states a relation once, in its frontmatter, under one of six keys: `part-of`
    (on the child), `blocks` (on the entry that must finish first), `duplicates`, `split-from`,
    `supersedes` and `relates`. Each takes one id or a block list of ids (`  - X-0012` lines under
    the key), read the way `artifacts:` is; an inline `[a, b]` list is not read here. The inverse is
    never written, only derived: `show` ends with a Relations block listing each relation the entry
    states and each one another entry states about it (children, blocked by, duplicated by, split
    into, superseded by, related by), each with the other entry's status. An id with a prefix other
    than this register's is looked up through the fleet manifest (`fleet-decl`: the project whose
    `leakPrefix` lists the prefix, and its `path`), when that tool and the manifest are present. A
    renamed register declares its old prefix beside its new one (`NEW,OLD`), and an id under either
    is matched by number, since a rename keeps numbers; otherwise it is shown as unresolved, and nothing fails because it could not be.

RELATED. `related <words...>` searches every entry, open and closed, for the words in its title,
    body and `artifacts:`, and prints id, status, title and the number of matches, most first. It is
    the lookup that runs before an entry is filed: a duplicate is found by one search or not at all.
    It exits 0 with "no match" when nothing matches, since that is an answer.

HEALTH REPORTS, each advisory. Besides the folder and cadence reports above, `health` prints
    `[untyped-mention]` (a body mention of another entry of this register with no typed relation
    between the two in either direction; a count, or the list with `--mentions`, because a register
    of hundreds of entries would otherwise print hundreds of lines), `[gate-closed]` (a `blocked` or
    `held` entry whose `gate:` names an entry that has closed), `[gate-untyped]` (such a `gate:`
    naming an entry that does not list the gated one under `blocks:`), `[child-of-closed]` (an open
    entry `part-of` a closed one), `[unresolved-foreign]` (ids in a relation key or a `gate:` that
    name another register and do not resolve, grouped by prefix with a count) and
    `standing-overdue` (a `standing` entry carrying `overdue: report`, past its cadence, which
    is what that field exempts from the register lint's blocking staleness check).

    It does not WRITE entries. Creating, editing and closing them is hand work against a
    contract each register states in its own README, and a tool that generated entries would
    be generating the closure conditions and resolutions that are the whole point of writing
    them by hand.
"""

import argparse
import collections
import datetime
import os
import pathlib
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys

CLOSED_STATUSES = ("done", "dropped")
# Days a `standing` entry may go unverified per declared cadence. The same table the register's
# lint holds; it is restated here because the two tools are installed separately.
CADENCE_DAYS = {"weekly": 7, "fortnightly": 14, "monthly": 31, "quarterly": 93, "biannual": 186, "annual": 366}
# An active initiative whose folder has had no commit for this long is reported by `health`.
QUIET_DAYS = 30
# The repository-wide type folders under docs/; a document here that no initiative claims is
# what `health` looks for. Mirrors the layout names the context lint knows.
TYPE_DIRS = ("specs", "plans", "runbooks", "research", "reference", "policies", "compliance")
# The six relation keys an entry may state, and how each reads from the other end.
RELATION_KEYS = ("part-of", "blocks", "duplicates", "split-from", "supersedes", "relates")
INVERSE = {"part-of": "children", "blocks": "blocked by", "duplicates": "duplicated by",
           "split-from": "split into", "supersedes": "superseded by", "relates": "related by"}
ID_TOKEN = re.compile(r"(?<![\w-])([A-Z][A-Z0-9]*-)(\d+)(?![\w])")
ORDER = ("active", "ready", "blocked", "held", "standing", "scoped", "idea", "done", "dropped")


class Entry:
    def __init__(self, path, closed):
        self.path = path
        self.closed = closed
        self.text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
        self.meta = {}
        if self.text.startswith("---"):
            end = self.text.find("\n---", 3)
            if end != -1:
                for line in self.text[3:end].splitlines():
                    if line.strip() and not line.startswith((" ", "\t", "-", "#")):
                        key, sep, value = line.partition(":")
                        if sep:
                            self.meta[key.strip()] = value.strip().strip("'\"")

    @property
    def folder(self):
        """The path of the entry's `folder:` artifact, or "". Read from the artifacts block, which
        the frontmatter reader above skips because its items are list lines."""
        if not self.text.startswith("---"):
            return ""
        end = self.text.find("\n---", 3)
        block = re.search(r"^artifacts:\s*$(.*?)(?=^\S|\Z)", self.text[3:end], re.M | re.S)
        if block:
            for value in re.findall(r"^\s*-\s*(.+?)\s*$", block.group(1), re.M):
                kind, sep, rest = value.strip("'\" ").partition(":")
                if sep and kind.strip() == "folder":
                    return rest.strip()
        return ""

    @property
    def frontmatter(self):
        end = self.text.find("\n---", 3) if self.text.startswith("---") else -1
        return self.text[3:end] if end != -1 else ""

    @property
    def body(self):
        end = self.text.find("\n---", 3) if self.text.startswith("---") else -1
        return self.text[end + 4:] if end != -1 else self.text

    def relations(self):
        """{key: [ids]} for the six relation keys, each a scalar or a block list of ids. Ids are
        found by shape, so a trailing comment or quoting on a line does not hide one."""
        out = {}
        lines = self.frontmatter.splitlines()
        for i, line in enumerate(lines):
            key, sep, value = line.partition(":")
            if not sep or key not in RELATION_KEYS:
                continue
            ids = [m.group(0) for m in ID_TOKEN.finditer(value.split(" #")[0])]
            for nxt in lines[i + 1:]:
                if re.match(r"^\s*-", nxt):
                    ids += [m.group(0) for m in ID_TOKEN.finditer(nxt.split(" #")[0])]
                elif nxt.strip() and not nxt.lstrip().startswith("#"):
                    break
            out[key] = ids
        return out

    @property
    def artifacts_text(self):
        block = re.search(r"^artifacts:\s*$(.*?)(?=^\S|\Z)", self.frontmatter, re.M | re.S)
        return block.group(1) if block else ""

    def get(self, key, default=""):
        return self.meta.get(key, default)

    @property
    def id(self):
        return self.get("id")

    @property
    def num(self):
        m = re.search(r"(\d+)$", self.id)
        return int(m.group(1)) if m else 0

    def section(self, heading):
        m = re.search(r"^#{1,6}\s+%s\s*$(.*?)(?=^#{1,6}\s|\Z)" % re.escape(heading),
                      self.text, re.M | re.S)
        return " ".join(m.group(1).split()) if m else ""


def load(root):
    """Every entry, actionable and closed. An entry is identified by CONTENT -- frontmatter
    carrying an id -- not by filename, so a register may hold a README, a mapping table or a
    charter without each being counted as a malformed entry."""
    out = []
    for sub, closed in ((root, False), (root / "closed", True)):
        if not sub.is_dir():
            continue
        for f in sorted(sub.glob("*.md")):
            e = Entry(f, closed)
            if e.id:
                out.append(e)
    out.sort(key=lambda e: e.num)
    return out


def select(entries, args):
    if args.status:
        wanted = {s.strip() for s in args.status.split(",")}
        entries = [e for e in entries if e.get("status") in wanted]
    if args.open:
        entries = [e for e in entries if e.get("status") not in CLOSED_STATUSES]
    if args.closed:
        entries = [e for e in entries if e.get("status") in CLOSED_STATUSES]
    if args.match:
        pat = re.compile(args.match, re.I)
        entries = [e for e in entries if pat.search(e.get("title")) or pat.search(e.id)]
    return entries


def repo_root(root):
    """The nearest ancestor of the register directory holding `.git` (a directory, or the file a
    worktree has), or None. None means "do not answer git questions", never "guess a root"."""
    root = root.resolve()
    for d in [root] + list(root.parents):
        if (d / ".git").exists():
            return d
    return None


def git_out(base, *argv):
    """Run git in `base` with git's own variables removed (see the docstring), or None on failure."""
    clean = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if not k.startswith("GIT_")}
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(base)] + list(argv), capture_output=True, text=True,
                           env=clean)
    except OSError:
        return None
    return r.stdout if r.returncode == 0 else None


def key_of(token):
    """(prefix, number) of an id string, so `ABC-7` and `ABC-0007` are the same entry."""
    m = ID_TOKEN.fullmatch(token)
    return (m.group(1), int(m.group(2))) if m else None


class Resolver:
    """Where an id in a relation points: an entry of this register, an entry of another register the
    fleet manifest lets this machine read, or nowhere it can be reached.

    The other registers are found through `fleet-decl` (the project declaring the id's `leakPrefix`,
    then its `path` under the Devel root), asked lazily so a register with only its own ids never
    calls it. Every failure -- no `fleet-decl`, no `yq`, no manifest, a register not cloned here --
    reads as "unresolved", never as an error: a relation is still true on a machine that cannot
    look it up."""

    def __init__(self, entries):
        self.own = {}
        for e in entries:
            k = key_of(e.id)
            if k:
                self.own[k] = e
        self.prefixes = {k[0] for k in self.own}
        self.paths = None
        self.loaded = {}

    def _decl(self, *argv):
        try:
            r = subprocess.run([self.tool] + list(argv), capture_output=True, text=True)
        except OSError:
            return ""
        return r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else ""

    def _paths(self):
        if self.paths is None:
            self.paths = {}
            self.tool = shutil.which("fleet-decl")
            if self.tool:
                devel = pathlib.Path(os.environ.get("FLEET_DEVEL_ROOT") or
                                     pathlib.Path.home() / "Devel")
                for project in self._decl("--projects-with", "leakPrefix").split():
                    prefixes = self._decl("--entry", project, "leakPrefix")
                    path = self._decl("--entry", project, "path")
                    for prefix in prefixes.replace(",", " ").split():
                        if path:
                            self.paths[prefix] = devel / path / "docs" / "register"
        return self.paths

    def foreign_register(self, prefix):
        """The register that owns `prefix` ("ABC-" style), as {number: entry}, or None. Keyed by
        number alone, so an id under a renamed register's old prefix finds the entry."""
        name = prefix.rstrip("-")
        if name not in self.loaded:
            path = self._paths().get(name)
            found = None
            if path is not None and path.is_dir():
                try:
                    found = {key_of(e.id)[1]: e for e in load(path) if key_of(e.id)}
                except OSError:
                    found = None
            self.loaded[name] = found
        return self.loaded[name]

    def resolve(self, token):
        """(kind, entry-or-None): kind is "own" or "foreign"; entry is None when it does not
        resolve. An own-prefix id with no entry is "own" and None -- a dangling relation."""
        k = key_of(token)
        if k is None:
            return ("foreign", None)
        if k[0] in self.prefixes:
            return ("own", self.own.get(k))
        reg = self.foreign_register(k[0])
        return ("foreign", reg.get(k[1]) if reg else None)


def is_closed(e):
    return e.get("status") in CLOSED_STATUSES


def relation_rows(e, entries, res):
    """(label, id, entry-or-None, note) rows for `show`: each relation `e` states, then each one
    another entry of this register states about `e`."""
    rows = []
    for key in RELATION_KEYS:
        for t in e.relations().get(key, []):
            kind, other = res.resolve(t)
            note = "" if other else (
                "unresolved: not an entry of this register" if kind == "own" else
                "unresolved: another register this machine cannot read")
            rows.append((key, t, other, note))
    mine = key_of(e.id)
    for x in entries:
        for key, targets in x.relations().items():
            if any(key_of(t) == mine for t in targets):
                rows.append((INVERSE[key], x.id, x, ""))
    return rows


def show_relations(e, entries, res):
    rows = relation_rows(e, entries, res)
    if not rows:
        return
    print("\nRelations")
    for label, token, other, note in rows:
        detail = ("[%s]  %s" % (other.get("status"), other.get("title"))) if other else "(%s)" % note
        print("  %-13s %-10s %s" % (label, token, detail))


def cmd_list(entries, args):
    if not entries:
        print("no entries match", file=sys.stderr)
        return 0
    width = max(len(e.id) for e in entries)
    fwidth = max(len(e.folder or "-") for e in entries)
    for e in entries:
        line = "%-*s  %-9s  %-*s  %s" % (width, e.id, e.get("status"), fwidth, e.folder or "-",
                                         e.get("title"))
        if args.long and e.get("gate"):
            line += "\n%s  gate: %s" % (" " * width, e.get("gate"))
        print(line)
    return 0


def cmd_related(entries, args):
    """Entries whose title, body or `artifacts:` contain any of the words, most matches first.
    Over every entry, closed ones included: a closed entry is the likeliest thing to duplicate."""
    words = [w.lower() for w in args.words]
    scored = []
    for e in entries:
        hay = "\n".join((e.get("title"), e.body, e.artifacts_text)).lower()
        n = sum(hay.count(w) for w in words)
        if n:
            scored.append((-n, e.num, e))
    if not scored:
        print("no match")
        return 0
    scored.sort(key=lambda t: t[:2])
    width = max(len(e.id) for _, _, e in scored)
    for neg, _, e in scored:
        print("%-*s  %-9s  %s  (%d)" % (width, e.id, e.get("status"), e.get("title"), -neg))
    return 0


def cmd_stats(entries, args):
    by = collections.Counter(e.get("status") for e in entries)
    total = sum(by.values())
    for status in ORDER:
        if by.get(status):
            print("%-9s %4d" % (status, by[status]))
    for status in sorted(set(by) - set(ORDER)):
        print("%-9s %4d  (outside the vocabulary)" % (status, by[status]))
    print("%-9s %4d" % ("total", total))
    return 0


def cmd_show(entries, args):
    hits = [e for e in entries if e.id == args.words[0]]
    if not hits:
        print("no entry with id %r" % args.words[0], file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    e = hits[0]
    sys.stdout.write(e.text)
    show_relations(e, entries, Resolver(entries))
    if e.folder:
        show_folder(e, args)
    return 0


def show_folder(e, args):
    """Append the entry's folder: its path, its files grouped by type subdirectory, and the
    decision records its README names. Read from disk, so a folder that is not there says so."""
    base = repo_root(pathlib.Path(args.root))
    print("\nfolder: %s" % e.folder)
    if base is None or not (base / e.folder).is_dir():
        print("  (not found on disk)")
        return
    top = base / e.folder
    groups = collections.defaultdict(list)
    for f in sorted(p for p in top.rglob("*") if p.is_file()):
        rel = f.relative_to(top)
        groups[rel.parts[0] + "/" if len(rel.parts) > 1 else "."].append("/".join(rel.parts[1:]) if len(rel.parts) > 1 else rel.name)
    for group in sorted(groups, key=lambda g: (g != ".", g)):
        print("  %s" % ("files directly in the folder" if group == "." else group))
        for name in groups[group]:
            print("    %s" % name)
    readme = top / "README.md"
    if readme.is_file():
        text = readme.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
        seen = []
        for m in re.finditer(r"\b[A-Z][A-Z0-9]*-ADR-\d+\b|(?:[\w./-]*/)?adr/[\w.-]+\.md", text):
            if m.group(0) not in seen:
                seen.append(m.group(0))
        if seen:
            print("  decision records its README names")
            for name in seen:
                print("    %s" % name)


def cmd_index(entries, args):
    """Render the browsable table. Written on demand, never a file the repo must keep fresh."""
    rows = ["<!-- GENERATED by `register index` — do not hand-edit. This file is a CONVENIENCE,",
            "     not a source: the entries are, and this can be regenerated at any time. -->",
            "# Register index",
            "",
            "One row per entry. Full detail is the linked entry.",
            "",
            "| ID | Status | Title | Gate |",
            "| --- | --- | --- | --- |"]
    for e in entries:
        rel = e.path.name if not e.closed else "closed/" + e.path.name
        title = e.get("title").replace("|", r"\|")
        gate = e.get("gate", "").replace("|", r"\|") or "—"
        rows.append("| [%s](%s%s) | %s | %s | %s |"
                    % (e.id, args.link_base, rel, e.get("status"), title, gate))
    text = "\n".join(rows) + "\n"
    if args.write:
        pathlib.Path(args.write).write_text(text, encoding="utf-8")
        print("wrote %s (%d entries)" % (args.write, len(entries)), file=sys.stderr)
    else:
        sys.stdout.write(text)
    return 0


def named_in(name, text):
    """Does `name` appear in `text` as a whole file name, not the tail of a longer one?"""
    return re.search(r"(?<![\w.-])" + re.escape(name) + r"(?![\w-])", text) is not None


def cmd_health(entries, args):
    """Advisory report of what has gone quiet or unowned. Always exits 0."""
    base = repo_root(pathlib.Path(args.root))
    findings = []
    now = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).timestamp()
    if base is None:
        print("register: %s is not inside a git repository -- skipping the folder and document "
              "checks" % args.root, file=sys.stderr)
    for e in entries:
        if base is not None and e.get("status") == "active" and e.folder:
            out = git_out(base, "log", "-1", "--format=%ct", "--", e.folder)
            last = out.strip() if out is not None else ""
            if not last:
                findings.append("%s: active, and its folder %s has no commit" % (e.id, e.folder))
            elif now - int(last) > QUIET_DAYS * 86400:
                findings.append("%s: active, and its folder %s has no commit in %d days"
                                % (e.id, e.folder, QUIET_DAYS))
        if e.get("status") == "standing":
            cadence, verified = e.get("cadence"), e.get("verified")
            if cadence in CADENCE_DAYS and re.match(r"^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$", verified):
                age = (datetime.date.today() - datetime.date.fromisoformat(verified)).days
                if age > CADENCE_DAYS[cadence]:
                    label = "standing-overdue" if e.get("overdue") == "report" else "standing"
                    findings.append("%s: %s, last verified %s, %dd ago, past its %s cadence"
                                    % (e.id, label, verified, age, cadence))
    findings += relation_findings(entries, args)
    if base is not None:
        findings += unclaimed_documents(base, entries)
    if not findings:
        print("health: nothing to report")
    for line in findings:
        print(line)
    return 0


def relation_findings(entries, args):
    """What the typed relations and the prose around them do not yet agree on. Advisory."""
    res = Resolver(entries)
    out = []
    typed = set()       # unordered pairs of entry keys joined by any relation
    for e in entries:
        for targets in e.relations().values():
            for t in targets:
                typed.add(frozenset((key_of(e.id), key_of(t))))
    mentions = []
    for e in entries:
        me, seen = key_of(e.id), set()
        for m in ID_TOKEN.finditer(e.body):
            k = (m.group(1), int(m.group(2)))
            other = res.own.get(k)
            if other is not None and k != me and k not in seen and frozenset((me, k)) not in typed:
                seen.add(k)
                mentions.append((e, other))
    if mentions:
        if args.mentions:
            out += ["[untyped-mention] %s mentions %s, with no typed relation between them"
                    % (e.id, o.id) for e, o in mentions]
        else:
            out.append("[untyped-mention] %d body mentions of another entry with no typed relation "
                       "(list them with --mentions)" % len(mentions))
    foreign = collections.defaultdict(set)
    for e in entries:
        if e.get("status") in ("blocked", "held"):
            for m in ID_TOKEN.finditer(e.get("gate")):
                kind, gated = res.resolve(m.group(0))
                if kind != "own" or gated is None or gated is e:
                    continue
                if is_closed(gated):
                    out.append("[gate-closed] %s: %s, gated on %s, which is %s"
                               % (e.id, e.get("status"), gated.id, gated.get("status")))
                if key_of(e.id) not in {key_of(t) for t in gated.relations().get("blocks", [])}:
                    out.append("[gate-untyped] %s: gated on %s, which does not list it under blocks"
                               % (e.id, gated.id))
        if not is_closed(e):
            for t in e.relations().get("part-of", []):
                kind, parent = res.resolve(t)
                if kind == "own" and parent is not None and is_closed(parent):
                    out.append("[child-of-closed] %s: open, part-of %s, which is %s"
                               % (e.id, parent.id, parent.get("status")))
        tokens = [t for ts in e.relations().values() for t in ts]
        tokens += [m.group(0) for m in ID_TOKEN.finditer(e.get("gate"))]
        for t in tokens:
            kind, other = res.resolve(t)
            if kind == "foreign" and other is None:
                foreign[key_of(t)[0].rstrip("-")].add(t)
    for prefix in sorted(foreign):
        out.append("[unresolved-foreign] %s: %d ids this machine cannot resolve (%s)"
                   % (prefix, len(foreign[prefix]), ", ".join(sorted(foreign[prefix]))))
    return out


def unclaimed_documents(base, entries):
    """Documents in the repository-wide type folders that no initiative names (or only one does).

    NAMED means the file name appears in a register entry, a decision record or a folder README.
    Whether it is claimed by an INITIATIVE is narrower: an entry, or that entry's folder README --
    counted once per initiative id, so an entry and its own README naming a document do not make
    two. A decision record or a subject README makes a document named without making it claimed."""
    docs = base / "docs"
    claimants = []      # (initiative id or None, text)
    for e in entries:
        claimants.append((e.id, e.text))
    for adr in sorted((docs / "adr").glob("*.md")) if (docs / "adr").is_dir() else []:
        claimants.append((None, adr.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")))
    readmes = sorted(docs.glob("*/README.md")) + sorted(docs.glob("initiatives/*/README.md")) \
        + sorted(docs.glob("archive/initiatives/*/README.md"))
    for r in readmes:
        m = re.match(r"^([A-Z][A-Z0-9]*-\d+)", r.parent.name)
        in_initiatives = r.parent.parent.name == "initiatives"
        claimants.append((m.group(1) if m and in_initiatives else None,
                          r.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")))
    out = []
    for t in TYPE_DIRS:
        for f in sorted(p for p in (docs / t).rglob("*") if p.is_file()) if (docs / t).is_dir() else []:
            if f.name == "README.md":
                continue
            rel = f.relative_to(base).as_posix()
            hits = [(i, text) for i, text in claimants if named_in(f.name, text)]
            initiatives = sorted({i for i, _ in hits if i})
            if not hits:
                out.append("%s: named by nothing -- no entry, decision record or README" % rel)
            elif len(initiatives) == 1:
                out.append("%s: named by one initiative (%s) only -- a candidate to move into its "
                           "folder" % (rel, initiatives[0]))
    return out


ID_SHAPE = re.compile(r"^(.*?)(\d+)$")


def cmd_next(entries, args):
    """Print the next free id. Only the id goes to stdout, so it can be captured by a script."""
    shapes = [ID_SHAPE.match(e.id) for e in entries]
    bad = [e.id for e, m in zip(entries, shapes) if not m]
    if bad:
        print("register: ids without a trailing number: %s" % ", ".join(bad), file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    prefixes = sorted({m.group(1) for m in shapes})
    if len(prefixes) != 1:
        # Derived, never configured: two prefixes means the store does not say which is its own.
        print("register: more than one id prefix (%s) -- cannot tell which is this register's"
              % ", ".join(prefixes), file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    widths = sorted({len(m.group(2)) for m in shapes})
    width = widths[-1]
    nxt = max(int(m.group(2)) for m in shapes) + 1
    if len(widths) > 1:
        print("register: ids here are mixed width (%s digits) -- a lexical listing of this "
              "register reports the wrong highest id; pad them to one width"
              % ", ".join(map(str, widths)), file=sys.stderr)
    if len(str(nxt)) > width:
        print("register: the next id is wider than every existing id -- from here a lexical "
              "listing disagrees with a numeric one; pad the register", file=sys.stderr)
    print("%s%0*d" % (prefixes[0], width, nxt))
    return 0


def main(argv=None):
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="Read a work register.")
    p.add_argument("command", choices=("list", "stats", "show", "index", "next", "health", "related"))
    p.add_argument("words", nargs="*", help="an entry id for `show`; the words for `related`")
    p.add_argument("--root", default="docs/register", help="the register directory")
    p.add_argument("--status", help="comma-separated statuses to include")
    p.add_argument("--open", action="store_true", help="actionable entries only")
    p.add_argument("--closed", action="store_true", help="finished entries only")
    p.add_argument("--match", help="regex over id and title")
    p.add_argument("--mentions", action="store_true", help="list untyped mentions, for `health`")
    p.add_argument("--long", action="store_true", help="include the gate, for `list`")
    p.add_argument("--write", help="write to this path, for `index`")
    p.add_argument("--link-base", default="", help="path prefix for links, for `index`")
    a = p.parse_args(argv)

    root = pathlib.Path(a.root)
    if not root.is_dir():
        print("register: no register at %s" % root, file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    entries = load(root)
    if not entries:
        # NOT an empty list quietly returned. A directory holding no readable entries is a fact
        # about the directory, and reporting it as "nothing matched" would hide a wrong --root.
        print("register: %s holds no entries -- check --root" % root, file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if a.command == "show":
        if not a.words:
            print("register show needs an id", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        return cmd_show(entries, a)
    if a.command == "related":
        if not a.words:
            print("register related needs at least one word", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        # Over EVERY entry: a closed entry is the likeliest duplicate.
        return cmd_related(entries, a)
    if a.command == "next":
        # Over EVERY entry: filters would hide the closed ids that must never be reused.
        return cmd_next(entries, a)
    if a.command == "health":
        # Over EVERY entry, like `next`: a filter would hide the claimants a document is named by.
        return cmd_health(entries, a)
    return {"list": cmd_list, "stats": cmd_stats, "index": cmd_index}[a.command](
        select(entries, a), a)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
